The function f() takes three parameters: the string $$$s$$$, the index $$$i$$$ (starting from 0), and the index $$$j$$$ (starting from the length of the string).

For $$$i$$$ = $$$j$$, the function simply checks if $$$s$$$[$$$i$$$] = $$$s$$$[$$$j$$$], which ensures that the characters at indexes $$$i$$$ and $$$j$$$ are the same. If they are, the function returns True.

For $$$i$$$ > $$$j$$, the function simply returns False.

For any other value of $$$i$$$, the function recursively calls itself with $$$i$$$+1 and $$$j$$$-1. It does this until it reaches the base case $$$i$$$$$ = $$$j$$, where the characters at indexes $$$i$$$ and $$$j$$$ need to be different.

If $$$i$$$ = $$$j$$$, the function returns False since the characters are the same.

